-- The two ways an L4 defined term cannot become a docassemble `auto terms:`
-- entry. Both used to be silent, and the fidelity report used to say
-- "(nothing lost)" over them.
--
--   * `s 12(1)` carries regex metacharacters. Docassemble interpolates an
--     `auto terms` key straight into a regular expression with no re.escape
--     (parse.py:2908 at 1b6678384), so a BALANCED `(...)` compiles to a
--     capture group whose pattern no longer matches the term that named it,
--     and an UNBALANCED one raises re.error while the Interview is being
--     constructed — the emitted interview then cannot be loaded at all, while
--     `l4 check` and `l4 docassemble` both report success. Expect
--     DA-GLOSS-REGEX and no glossary entry.
--
--   * `notice` folds onto `Notice` under docassemble's own key normalisation
--     (lower-case + whitespace-collapse, parse.py:2905), so only one of the two
--     can be a key at all — and what the loser costs is not a duplicate key but
--     its whole definition. Expect DA-GLOSS-COLLIDE, `Notice`'s gloss kept
--     (first spelling wins, deterministically), and `notice`'s gone.

@desc A formal notice served under the Act
DECLARE Notice
    HAS
      @desc whether the notice was served
      `served` IS A BOOLEAN
      @desc whether the deadline has passed
      `deadline passed` IS A BOOLEAN

@export Whether the notice rule is met
GIVEN n IS A Notice @desc The notice under assessment
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `notice rule met` n IF `s 12(1)` AND `notice`
 WHERE
    @desc REGEXDROPPEDGLOSS the served requirement of the section
    `s 12(1)` MEANS n's `served`

    @desc COLLIDEDROPPEDGLOSS a completely different meaning from the type's
    `notice` MEANS n's `deadline passed`

#EVAL `notice rule met` OF Notice WITH `served` IS TRUE, `deadline passed` IS TRUE
-- TRUE
